  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Isoelectronic species refers to two atoms, ions or molecules that have the same electronic structure and the same number of valence electrons term means &quot;equal electric&quot; or &quot;equal charge&quot;. Isoelectronic chemical species typically display similar chemical properties.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"}\" open=\"\"><mtable columnalign=\"left\"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>Sm</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#8594;</mo><mi>electron</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>Er</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#8594;</mo><mi>electron</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>65</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Tb</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#8594;</mo><mi>electron</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>63</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>Tm</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#8594;</mo><mi>electron</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>65</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></mfenced></math>&nbsp;(not isoelectronic)</p><p>While&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Yb</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Lu</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;both will have 68 electrons. and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Eu</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Tb</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;both will have 61 electrons.so, they are isoelectronic with each other.</p><p>Note: According to NTA&nbsp;only option D is given as answer but option A and D both will be the correct answer.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></blockquote>"
  }
